finition of  what to do when it  finds a java app and it is now  finishing up its build and it has  successfully done and created this hello  build packer image so now i can just run  that  and we'll bind it to port 80 so we can  load it in our web browser this is a  server that i have  up in gce and that was hello bill packer  and it runs and there we go  it started and now we go over to our web  browser this is the address of my server  it wasn't working before but if i do a  reload  boom hello buildpacker it works and so  you can see in just  a couple of seconds we built a java app  and got it running running  in a docker container and that is  uh a quick demo of build packs  so that was built packs in action now  build packs allow you to take advantage  of the benefits of containers without  needing to understand them  so you can isolate your code you can  create microservices architectures you  can run on  orchestration platforms like kubernetes  without needing to write docker files or  understand the inner organs of docker  so super cool now recently google cloud  uh released a set of open source build  packs you can find them on github at the  address that you see in this screenshot  google cloud platform slash build packs  and these cover  a bunch of the most popular languages go  javascript  java php python  and ruby and they are  designed to create secure container  images which can run  on any of the runtimes from google cloud  such as cloud run gke or anthos  and of course because they're open  source you're able to fork them and use  them  however you'd like now even though  they're relatively new  these build packs have powered over 10  million  builds in production and that's because  they started off as an implementation  detail  for app engine and uh google cloud  functions  and they've been uh powering  all of these source builds on those two  products uh for a while now  so we're pretty confident in their  robustness  and you can use them confidently too in  production  but in addition to app engine cloud  functions google cloud  build packs are available on many of our  products uh their support for them in  cloud build cloud run  cloud shell cloud code and scaffold  and we'll dive into the specifics of  that later on  so that was the overview let's dive in  what are build packs exactly uh they are  a cncf sandbox project those are newer  projects  in the uh cloud native uh project and  you can find them at buildpacks.io  uh there are several uh large companies  contributing to the project  in addition to google um pivotal cloud  foundry  is a major backer as is salesforce uh  via the heroku division  and like we said before build packs take  your source code and they  create a container but there's a little  more to them than this and let's take a  look  at that build packs actually  run in a container themselves called a  builder  so here you can see in a little more  detail  that uh a a container called the builder  runs the build packs um and you can see  there's actually two scripts they run  the first one is a detect script and  that detects what language  your code is and um the the detection  it's actually not a rocket science it's  an ordered list  of um tests and the first test that  comes back positive  uh is declared the language so these  tests can be very simple  like is there a packages.json and if  that's true then it's a javascript  application and you can of course  override those but you'll find that  they're  they usually work just fine and then  after the detection script happens  a build step happens that actually does  the building  so the builder it's itself an oci image  um it contains all the build packs and  groups and binaries needed to do the  building  and it runs on a platform uh and that  could be as simple as the pax cli  command that you saw in the demo  or something like cloud builder techton  and that actually  runs the uh the image and you can see  here a little cli snapshot  the pac command if you uh invoke suggest  builders  it'll show you some of the builders that  are uh known to it  um and you know google's one of those uh  this is also showing picado which are  the  that's the branding of the build packs  uh from pivotal that were released  recently they're also very good  and just to add another layer um there's  also a thing called a build pack group  uh build packs themselves are very small  units of execution they can be designed  to do just one thing like run an npm  install you know just installing  dependencies  and a build pack group is a collection  of those and that lets you reuse  smaller bits of code um like doing a  dependency install  and then and use those across different  build pack groups so  when most people say build pack they're  actually talking about the build pack  group that does the full build  um but it's uh it depends how the build  packs are actually designed  finally there is another piece of the  build pack called the stack  and this is the base image or base layer  that your application code is put on top  of here we  show an ubuntu stack which is what  google cloud's built packs use  and they can contain some system  libraries  on top of that base layer and then your  application code and dependencies  get put at the at the top of that layer  stack  um build packs cannot do an apt-get to  install dependencies  uh so those need to be part of the stack  and we have designed the stacks for the  cloud build packs  to contain the most common things that  you likely need  so you shouldn't have to redefine that  if you're using them yourself  so string all of that together we can  see here  a flow diagram where the source code  gets taken into the builder image uh  the build packs and build pack groups  run on it they run the detect script to  figure out which language it is  they run the build script uh they  install all of your  dependencies your source code whether  it's compiled or just direct goes on top  of there  and that gets layered on top of the  stack and  and then uh that layer cake that you see  on the right  the green and the gray uh that forms the  complete  container image which you can then run  wherever you'd like  so that's a pretty detailed look into  how the build pack works  so why would you actually want to use  these things  well for starters a recent study  found that 44 of containers in the wild  had known vulnerabilities for which  patches existed  this just shows that writing your own  docker file it can actually be quite  fraught with errors  unless you're an expert or maybe have a  platform team that  provides stalker files for you so using  build packs  lets you circumvent that danger uh uh  assuming the build packs are designed  with security in mind  um now uh google cloud build packs  are just that they've been security  audited and they're constantly updated  uh making sure that they will not  produce uh vulnerabilities or certainly  at the bottom layer of the stack the  base layer uh doesn't have any uh  security issues that we're aware of  uh the containers are built according to  um our container best practices  uh call that the google way uh as much  as we can via the  via build packs um and uh  all these things let you like container  novices uh deploy to container platforms  like kubernetes uh without needing to  write their own docker files um and you  know even if your organization  um is uh has adopted docker maybe that  that adoption is not even and there are  some teams  that uh haven't yet adopted it but they  could take advantage of centralized  platforms for running their code  another use for build packs even if you  do know daca really well is if you just  need to do a quick and easy build maybe  you're  checking out an open source project or  some code  that you don't expect to be using long  term and you don't want to write a  docker file from scratch for it  so just use a build pack uh build it try  it out  and then boom you're done some specific  google cloud use cases  uh for uh build packs um because we're  using build packs to power the builds on  app engine or cloud functions  uh if you wanna migrate away from those  platforms to one of our others like  cloud run  or gke you can take those app engine and  cloud function build packs  uh you could continue to run them  against your source code  and have the same build process  happening  on your new platform and as you know  when you're migrating keeping as many  things constant  as possible is very very helpful to  minimize the disruption from that  migration  finally another reason to use build  packs  is because they are so well integrated  into so many parts of google cloud  like cloud build that um you can sort of  reuse that  the knowledge and those tools um across  many parts  of your cloud platform so now let's take  a look at  the support for build packs on google  cloud first of all  uh we already went over the open source  build packs um you are free to  fork those um modify them however you  like  uh it's quite easy to do and in the  readme  you'll find uh documentation how to do  that um cloud build  um which is our builder as a service  uh now has native support for build  packs um so  if you have source code and want it  built instead of running  build packs locally you can just uh push  that to cloud build and we'll build it  for you  uh at the moment uh that uh support is  cli only  uh but uh support in the console uh will  be coming soon  uh cloud run our next generation  serverless platform  uh supports direct source deployments  with buildpacks  so you can just do a cloud run deploy  push your source code  and it will build and deploy that all in  one step  uh you can also set up continuous  integration  with both cloud build and cloud run  where every time you update  your source repository it will do  a fresh build of that source using  buildplex  build packs and deploy to cloud run uh  cloud 